6 strata means 6 p-values, and at least one will
look significant by chance. They are NOT corrected for that here,
deliberately: these tests are strongly correlated - the same models, the
same folds, overlapping evidence - so a Bonferroni factor calibrated for
independent tests would be wrong in the other direction. Treat a
per-stratum p-value as descriptive, pointing at where a difference
concentrates, and the overall paired test as the confirmatory one. Do not
quote a band p-value as a headline; picking the band after seeing the
result is how a significant finding gets manufactured.

